{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>That you may learn to fear.</b> For there you will find the priests and the Levites, teachers of the Torah, and they will teach you the service and the fear. And they said in the tractate of Ta'anit: עשר תעשר — 'tithe' — R. Yochanan said: עשר בשביל שתתעשר — 'tithe so that you may become rich.' Now there is no expression of wealth in the language of Scripture, for both שיני\"ן (shins) are with a שוא, but R. Yochanan was explaining thus: 'tithe so that you may become rich from it many times over' — and this is the wealth; thus the Ra'avad, of blessed memory, wrote there in his commentary. Now this is certainly a test, and the Torah said (Deuteronomy 6) לא תנסו — 'you shall not test.' Rather, the Sages, of blessed memory, already expounded it otherwise, as it is said (Malachi 3:10): הביאו את כל המעשר אל בית האוצר ויהי טרף בביתי ובחנוני נא בזאת וגו' — 'Bring all the tithe into the storehouse… and test Me now in this, etc.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: עשר תעשר — 'tithe' — שלא תתחסר — 'so that you not lack' — a hint to those who count years to separate one of ten for the laborers of Torah. עשר תעשר — in the way of the world, a man who has a field gives half, a third, or a quarter; but the Holy One, blessed be He, is not so: He draws up winds, raises clouds, brings down rains, brings up dews, fattens fruits, and asks only one of ten; therefore Moses warned Israel: עשר תעשר. A story of one who used to separate his tithes properly — he had a field that would produce a thousand measures, and he would separate from it a hundred measures for tithe, and from the remainder he and his household were sustained all his days. When he died, he said to his son: 'Give your attention to this field; thus and so do I separate for tithe, and from it I was sustained all my days.' The first year the son sowed it; it produced a thousand measures; he separated from it a hundred measures of tithe. The second year he sowed it; it produced a thousand measures; the evil eye entered and it diminished by ten, for he did not give a hundred measures but ninety. The third year he sowed it; it diminished by a hundred — he lacked ten. The fourth year the field diminished by two hundred — he lacked ten. And so the fifth and sixth, until the field stood at its tithes. When his relatives saw this, they donned white garments and wrapped themselves in white and came to him. He said to them: 'Why have you come to rejoice over that man who was ostracized?' They said: 'Heaven forbid! We have come only to rejoice. Previously you were the master of the house and the Holy One, blessed be He, was the priest; and now you have become the priest and the Holy One, blessed be He, is the master of the house.' And therefore Moses warns Israel: עשר תעשר את כל תבואת זרעך — 'you shall tithe all the produce of your seed.'" "<b>The yield of the field.</b> They said in the Midrash: if you merit, the end is that you go forth to sow the field; and if not, 'the yield of the field' — it will taunt you. 'The yield of the field' — this is Esau, as it is said (Genesis 25:29): ״ויבא עשו מן השדה״." "<b>The produce of your seed.</b> If you merit, your end is סופך נפיק לחקלך וחמי עלמא צריך מטרא ותתפלל עליו והוא יוצא — 'you will go out to your field, and the world needs rain, and you will pray over it, and it will come forth' — this is 'the yield of the field'; and if not, the end is that the haters of Israel go forth to bury their dead in the field." "<b>The tithe of your grain and your wine.</b> If you tithe — דגנך, 'your grain'; and if not — דגני, 'My grain.' If you tithe — תירושך, 'your wine'; and if not — תירושי, 'My wine,' as it is said (Hosea 2:11): לכן אשוב ולקחתי דגני בעתו ותירושי במועדו — 'Therefore I will return and take My grain in its time and My wine in its season.' שנה שנה, year by year — Scripture teaches you that they do not tithe from one year for another, from the new upon the old; thus the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded." "<b>And the firstborn of your herd.</b> Not that Israelites eat them, for the firstborn is eaten by the priests." "<b>And know</b> that the firstborn has sanctity forever, and even nowadays outside the Land it has sanctity: it is not shorn and is not worked with, and it is forbidden to eat except when it has a blemish. And this is contrary to the opinion of the Rambam, of blessed memory, who wrote that the commandment of the firstborn of a clean animal applies only in the Land of Israel. And the Ra'avad, of blessed memory, already took him to task in his objections and said: even Rabbi Akiva, who held thus, said so only regarding the matter that it is not offered, but certainly it has the sanctity of a firstborn even nowadays outside the Land, and one may not permit it to be eaten unless it has a blemish. And the proof is from what they said in Sanhedrin: Rabbi Hiyya said to Rav bar Aha, 'When you descend to Babylonia, rule — rule; release firstborns — do not release' — and Babylonia is outside the Land. And furthermore in the chapter Pesulei ha-Mukdashin: that shepherd who came before Rav Ashi — he said, 'What need we be concerned for? Whether he is a priest or an Israelite, here is a firstborn and its blemish with it.' And it is known that Rav Ashi was in Babylonia, and this is a proof that the firstborn applies outside the Land to be eaten in its blemish. And furthermore in tractate Beitzah, chapter Ein Tzadin, concerning that man who brought a firstborn before Rava and had his head covered — he said, 'Remove its eye and examine it for its blemish.' He said to him, 'Go today and come tomorrow.' He said to him, 'What was the procedure?' He said to him, 'They would place a thorn on this side, so that as it stuck its head into the chamber to urinate, it would scratch against it and split it.' And this is a proof that our Sages in Babylonia were very scrupulous regarding the firstborn in its sanctity and in the matter of an induced blemish, and they would not do so except because it has sanctity, even outside the Land."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:45.349743328Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}